// The four masterclass beats: feel the method first, the of-not-for mechanics
// (explained for people meeting them for the first time — copy book §7's
// tea-and-blanket picture), the role you play while someone sounds, and the
// honest fit with existing work. Plus the whiteboard figure: the method is a
// structured, teachable thing, and the page should show that, not just say it.
const items = [
  {
    title: 'The practice, in your own body',
    body: 'A guided sounding practice — the sound of the moment, and the question under the whole method: what is below that? You feel what acknowledgment does before we talk about how it works — often as concretely as a spontaneous yawn, or a breath that deepens by itself: a nervous system changing state. Your own body is the only place this work can be understood from.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Why it works: the sound has to be theirs',
    body: 'Most sound work makes sound for you — the singing bowls, the sound bath — and you receive it. This is the reverse: the person makes the sound of what’s actually there. Like the difference between handing a friend tea and asking what happened — both are kind, but only one brings the thing out. The healing is in that acknowledgment; acknowledged things leave on their own. Which is why nobody, including you, can do it for them. And it isn’t catharsis: nobody screams here. Volume is a dial, not a dare.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Your role while they sound',
    body: 'Keep the room steady, ask the next honest question, and get out of the way — present without fixing, steering, or taking it on. Your own state is part of the room: a settled nervous system beside someone is what helps theirs settle enough to open — co-regulation, doing its quiet work. When real emotion arrives, you have a concrete move: the energy goes into the voice while the body stays soft. And when they discover what the sound was about, you leave that discovery to them.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Where it fits in your work — and where it doesn’t',
    body: 'How sounding sits underneath one-to-one sessions, groups, and rooms that have gone tense — it layers onto what you already do; it doesn’t replace it. The method’s edges, named plainly: what these 100 minutes equip you to hold, and what they don’t. And the honest part: this is not therapy, and it doesn’t replace yours.',
  },
];
---

<section class="we-what" id="inside">
  <div class="we-what-inner">
    <div class="we-what-head">
      <div class="we-eyebrow">Inside the 100 minutes</div>
      <h2 class="we-h2">
        First you feel it.<br />
        <em>Then we look at how it works.</em>
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="we-what-intro">
      <p>
        Somatic Vocal Healing is sounding, not singing: one honest tone, of
        what's actually there — a feeling, a body part, a moment. No melody, no
        performance, no talent required, for you or for the people you work
        with. It's a coherent, teachable method: it works through the body
        rather than the story, and the safety is built into the instrument
        itself — the voice self-titrates, never opening more than a person can
        be with. The masterclass is a structured look at how it holds together.
      </p>
      <p>
        We start in your own body, because that's where the method starts.
        Then we begin to listen as a practitioner does: what your role in the room becomes
        when the participant is the one doing the healing.
      </p>
